  "summary": "Eight preprints address noise, concentration and training costs in quantum learning via fixed circuits, calibration, ensembles and physics-informed kernels.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is reducing the burden of training variational quantum models. Several works avoid or limit parametrized-circuit optimization: a fixed multi-layer quantum extreme learning machine with a linear readout fitted via MMD for generative modeling, and Soft-MoQE, which gates fixed quantum feature maps classically and matches trainable QNNs on MNIST. Others target trainability directly through reusable calibration against hardware noise, Bagging and AdaBoost for variational regressors, and contractive layers with one trainable parameter on Rydberg arrays. An empirical study examines how entanglement, locality and bandwidth scaling control exponential concentration in quantum kernels and extreme learning. Physics-oriented applications include an inverse participation ratio kernel for quantum phases and JEPA-based world models inferring Hamiltonian parameters from few-body dynamics. All items are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
